private FoodItem spawnFood() {
    Point position;
    do {
        position = new Point(random.nextInt(board.getHeight()), random.nextInt(board.getWidth()));
    } while (occupied.contains(position));
    return foodSpawnStrategy.nextFoodCreator().spawnAt(position);
}

An earlier version of this line read:

    occupied.contains(newHead) && !(newHead.equals(tail) && !eatsFood)

The '&& !eatsFood' was later removed. Why was removing it safe?,Because eatsFood is always false when the head reaches the tail by coincidence,"Because spawnFood() never places food on an occupied cell, and the tail is occupied - so food can never sit on the tail, making 'newHead equals the tail' and 'eatsFood' mutually exclusive already",Because eating no longer affects the tail,It was not safe

