The tail is captured with body.peekLast() before any modification, and used later for both the collision check and the removal. Why capture it up front rather than calling peekLast() again where it is needed?,To avoid the cost of a second method call,"Because the body is modified in between - after addFirst the last element may no longer be the tail that the collision decision was based on, so the two uses must refer to the same original tail",Because peekLast() removes the element,Because tail must be final to be used in a boolean expression,,2,Both uses need the board as it was BEFORE this move. Re-reading afterwards would silently mix pre-move and post-move views of the same snake.,2

Real code:

protected SnakeGame(Board board, Point startPosition,
                    IFoodSpawnStrategy foodSpawnStrategy, Random random) {
    this.board = board;
    this.foodSpawnStrategy = foodSpawnStrategy;
    this.random = random;
    body.addFirst(startPosition);
    occupied.add(startPosition);
    this.currentFood = spawnFood();
}

The constructor adds the start position to both body and occupied before calling spawnFood(). Would swapping those two lines matter?,No - the order of independent statements never matters,"Yes. spawnFood() rejects positions already in occupied, so if it ran first the very first food could be placed directly under the snake's head",Yes - the snake would start with zero length,"No, because the board is empty at construction time",,2,"The one-cell snake is easy to overlook, and on a small board the chance of the food landing on it is not small. Ordering is doing real work in what looks like plain setup.",2

