A snake occupying (3,4) (3,3) (3,2) is travelling right. The player presses LEFT, which would put the head at (3,3). What happens, and is any special anti-reverse rule needed?,Nothing happens

Real code:

public abstract class SnakeGame {

    private final Deque<Point> body = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Set<Point> occupied = new HashSet<>();

    private IGameState state = RunningState.getInstance();
    private FoodItem currentFood;
    private int score = 0;
}

public class Point {
    private final int row;
    private final int col;

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) { ... row == point.row && col == point.col ... }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(row, col); }
}

Point overrides equals() and hashCode(). What in the snake's movement code would break if hashCode() were left out?,Nothing - equals() alone is sufficient,"The HashSet would stop working: two Points with the same row and column could land in different buckets, so occupied.contains(newHead) could report false for a cell the snake is genuinely on",The Deque would reject duplicate points,Only the printed output would look wrong,,2,Every use of occupied depends on value equality working in a hash-based collection. Overriding one without the other is the classic way to get a Set that appears to lose its contents.,2

